window.WORLD_BAKED = {"Edward Jenner":{"extract":"Edward Jenner (17 May 1749 – 26 January 1823) was an English physician and scientist who pioneered the concept of vaccines and created the smallpox vaccine, the world's first vaccine. The terms vaccine and vaccination are derived from Variolae vaccinae (\"pustules of the cow\"), the term devised by Jenner to denote cowpox.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_Jenner","img":"img/world/edward-jenner.jpg","thumb":"img/world/edward-jenner.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"John Raphael Smith","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Jenner.jpg"}},"English Civil War":{"extract":"The English Civil War or Great Rebellion was a series of civil wars and political machinations between Royalists and Parliamentarians in the Kingdom of England from 1642 to 1651. Part of the wider 1639 to 1653 Wars of the Three Kingdoms, the struggle consisted of the First English Civil War and the Second English Civil War.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/English_Civil_War","img":"img/world/english-civil-war.jpg","thumb":"img/world/english-civil-war.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Unknown authorUnknown author","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Naseby,_hand-coloured_copper_engraving.jpg"}},"Great Famine (Ireland)":{"extract":"The Great Famine, also known as the Great Hunger (Irish: an Gorta Mór [ənˠ ˈɡɔɾˠt̪ˠə ˈmˠoːɾˠ]), the Famine and the Irish Potato Famine, was a period of mass starvation and disease in Ireland from 1845 to 1852. With an excess death impact of 775,000 to 1.5 million, and related mass emigration of 1 million or more, it constituted a major historical social crisis and had a significant impact on Irish society and history.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Famine_(Ireland)","img":"img/world/great-famine-ireland.jpg","thumb":"img/world/great-famine-ireland.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"James Mahony","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skibbereen_by_James_Mahony,_1847.JPG"}},"United States Declaration of Independence":{"extract":"The Declaration of Independence, headed The unanimous Declaration of the thirteen united States of America on the document directed to be engrossed by Congress on July 19, 1776, is the founding document of the United States. On July 4, 1776, it was adopted unanimously by the Second Continental Congress, who were convened at the Pennsylvania State House, later renamed Independence Hall, in Philadelphia.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Declaration_of_Independence","img":"img/world/united-states-declaration-of-independence.jpg","thumb":"img/world/united-states-declaration-of-independence.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"original: w:Second Continental Congress; reproduction: William Stone","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:United_States_Declaration_of_Independence.jpg"}},"Napoleonic Wars":{"extract":"The Napoleonic Wars (1803–1815) were a global series of conflicts fought by shared European coalitions against the French First Republic (1803–1804) under the First Consul followed by the First French Empire (1804–1815) under the Emperor of the French, Napoleon I. The wars originated in political forces arising from the French Revolution (1789–1799) and French Revolutionary Wars (1792–1802) and produced a period of French domination over continental Europe. The wars are categorised as seven conflicts, five named after the coalitions that fought Napoleon, plus two named for their respective theatres (where they took place): the War of the Third Coalition, War of the Fourth Coalition, War of the Fifth Coalition, War of the Sixth Coalition, War of the Seventh Coalition, the Peninsular War, and the French invasion of Russia.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Napoleonic_Wars","img":"img/world/napoleonic-wars.jpg","thumb":"img/world/napoleonic-wars.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Henri Félix Emmanuel Philippoteaux","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charge_of_the_French_Cuirassiers_at_Waterloo.jpg"}},"Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica":{"extract":"Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica (English: The Mathematical Principles of Natural Philosophy), often called simply the Principia (), is a book by Sir Isaac Newton that expounds Newton's laws of motion and his law of universal gravitation.\nThe Principia is written in Latin and comprises three volumes, and was authorized (imprimatur) by Samuel Pepys, then-president of the Royal Society on 5 July 1686 and first published in 1687.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philosophi%C3%A6_Naturalis_Principia_Mathematica","img":"img/world/philosophi-naturalis-principia-mathematica.jpg","thumb":"img/world/philosophi-naturalis-principia-mathematica.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"The original uploader was Zhaladshar at English Wikisource.","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Newton%27s_Principia_title_page.png"}},"California gold rush":{"extract":"The California gold rush (1848–1855) was a gold rush in California, which began on January 24, 1848, when gold was found by James W. Marshall at Sutter's Mill in Coloma, California. The news of gold brought approximately 300,000 people from the rest of the United States and abroad to California, which had recently been conquered from Mexico.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/California_gold_rush","img":"img/world/california-gold-rush.jpg","thumb":"img/world/california-gold-rush.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Unknown authorUnknown author","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1850_Woman_and_Men_in_California_Gold_Rush.jpg"}},"Year Without a Summer":{"extract":"The year 1816 is known as the Year Without a Summer because of severe climate abnormalities that caused average global temperatures to decrease by 0.4–0.7 °C (0.7–1 °F). Summer temperatures in Europe that year were the coldest of any on record between 1766 and 2000, resulting in crop failures and major food shortages across the Northern Hemisphere.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Year_Without_a_Summer","img":"img/world/year-without-a-summer.jpg","thumb":"img/world/year-without-a-summer.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Caspar David Friedrich","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich,_Caspar_David_-_M%C3%B6nch_am_Meer_-_Alte_Nationalgalerie_in_Berlin.jpg"}},"Daguerreotype":{"extract":"Daguerreotype was the first publicly available photographic process, widely used from 1830s to the year 1850. \"Daguerreotype\" also refers to an image created through this process.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daguerreotype","img":"img/world/daguerreotype.jpg","thumb":"img/world/daguerreotype.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_Daguerre_2.jpg"}},"Ninety-five Theses":{"extract":"The  Ninety-five Theses or  Disputation on the Power and Efficacy of Indulgences is a list of propositions for an academic disputation written in 1517 by Martin Luther, then a professor of moral theology at the University of Wittenberg, Germany. The Theses are retrospectively considered to have launched the Protestant Reformation and the birth of Protestantism, despite various quasi- or proto-Protestant groups having existed previously.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ninety-five_Theses","img":"img/world/ninety-five-theses.jpg","thumb":"img/world/ninety-five-theses.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Martin Luther","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luther_95_Thesen.png"}},"1755 Lisbon earthquake":{"extract":"The 1755 Lisbon earthquake, also known as the Great Lisbon earthquake, occurred in the Iberian Peninsula and Northwest Africa area on the morning of Saturday, 1 November, 1755 (on the Christian Feast of All Saints); it took place at approximately 09:40 local time. In combination with subsequent fires and a tsunami, the earthquake almost completely destroyed Lisbon and adjoining areas.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1755_Lisbon_earthquake","img":"img/world/1755-lisbon-earthquake.jpg","thumb":"img/world/1755-lisbon-earthquake.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"USGS","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1755_Lisbon_Earthquake_Location.png"}},"Storming of the Bastille":{"extract":"The Storming of the Bastille (French: Prise de la Bastille [pʁiz də la bastij]), also known as Fall of the Bastille, which occurred in Paris, France, on 14 July 1789, was an act of political violence by revolutionary insurgents who attempted to storm and seize control of the medieval armoury, fortress, and political prison known as the Bastille. After four hours of fighting and 94 deaths, the insurgents were able to enter the Bastille.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Storming_of_the_Bastille","img":"img/world/storming-of-the-bastille.jpg","thumb":"img/world/storming-of-the-bastille.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Unidentified painter","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anonymous_-_Prise_de_la_Bastille.jpg"}},"Locomotion No. 1":{"extract":"Locomotion No. 1 (originally named Active) is an early steam locomotive that was built in 1825 by the pioneering railway engineers George and Robert Stephenson at their manufacturing firm, Robert Stephenson and Company.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Locomotion_No._1"},"Spanish Armada":{"extract":"The Spanish Armada (often known as Invincible Armada, or the Enterprise of England, Spanish: Grande y Felicísima Armada, lit. 'Great and Most Fortunate Navy') was a Spanish fleet that sailed from Lisbon in late May 1588, and was the largest engagement of the undeclared Anglo-Spanish War. The Armada was commanded by Alonso de Guzmán, Duke of Medina Sidonia, an aristocrat appointed by Philip II of Spain.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spanish_Armada","img":"img/world/spanish-armada.jpg","thumb":"img/world/spanish-armada.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Robert Adams, Augustine Ryther","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armadaroute.jpg"}},"Voyages of Christopher Columbus":{"extract":"Between 1492 and 1504, the Italian explorer and navigator Christopher Columbus led four transatlantic maritime expeditions to the Americas in the name of the Catholic Monarchs of Spain. These voyages led to Europeans learning about the New World and were an early breakthrough in the period known in Europe as the Age of Exploration, which saw the colonization of the Americas, a related biological exchange, and the beginning of trans-Atlantic trade.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voyages_of_Christopher_Columbus","img":"img/world/voyages-of-christopher-columbus.jpg","thumb":"img/world/voyages-of-christopher-columbus.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"John Vanderlyn","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Landing_of_Columbus_(2)_(cropped).jpg"}},"Great Fire of London":{"extract":"The Great Fire of London occurred from Sunday 2 September to Wednesday 5 September 1666, gutting the medieval City of London inside the old Roman city wall, while also extending past the wall to the west. The death toll is generally thought to have been relatively small, although some historians have challenged this belief.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Fire_of_London","img":"img/world/great-fire-of-london.jpg","thumb":"img/world/great-fire-of-london.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Unknown artistUnknown artist","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Fire_London.jpg"}},"Revolutions of 1848":{"extract":"The revolutions of 1848, also known as the springtime of the peoples, were a series of revolutions throughout Europe that spanned almost two years, between January 1848 and October 1849. They remain the most widespread revolutionary wave in European history to date.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Revolutions_of_1848","img":"img/world/revolutions-of-1848.jpg","thumb":"img/world/revolutions-of-1848.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Horace Vernet","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Horace_Vernet-Barricade_rue_Soufflot.jpg"}},"First voyage of James Cook":{"extract":"The first voyage of James Cook was a combined Royal Navy and Royal Society expedition to the south Pacific Ocean aboard HMS Endeavour, from 1768 to 1771. The aims were to observe the 1769 transit of Venus from Tahiti and to seek evidence of the postulated Terra Australis Incognita or \"undiscovered southern land\".","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_voyage_of_James_Cook","img":"img/world/first-voyage-of-james-cook.jpg","thumb":"img/world/first-voyage-of-james-cook.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Thomas Luny","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Bark,_Earl_of_Pembroke,_later_Endeavour,_leaving_Whitby_Harbour_in_1768_(cropped).jpg"}},"Mayflower":{"extract":"Mayflower was an English square-rigged merchant sailing ship, active from before 1609 until 1622. Her tonnage was between 180–200, and she was 110 feet (34 meters) long and 25 feet (7.5 meters) in the beam, with several decks.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mayflower","img":"img/world/mayflower.jpg","thumb":"img/world/mayflower.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Unknown authorUnknown author","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Copyrighted_and_Published_by_A_S_Burbank,_The_Mayflower_at_Sea_(NBY_21340).jpg"}},"Coronation of Queen Victoria":{"extract":"The coronation of Victoria as queen of the United Kingdom took place on Thursday, 28 June 1838, just over a year after she succeeded to the throne of the United Kingdom at the age of 18. The ceremony was held in Westminster Abbey after a public procession through the streets from Buckingham Palace, to which the Queen returned later as part of a second procession.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coronation_of_Queen_Victoria","img":"img/world/coronation-of-queen-victoria.jpg","thumb":"img/world/coronation-of-queen-victoria.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"George Hayter","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coronation_portrait_of_Queen_Victoria_1838.jpg"}},"American Civil War":{"extract":"The American Civil War (April 12, 1861 – May 26, 1865; also known by other names) was fought in the United States between the Union (\"the North\") and the Confederacy (\"the South\"), which was formed in 1861 by Southern states that had seceded from the Union to preserve the institution of slavery. The South saw slavery as threatened because of the election of Abraham Lincoln as president in 1860, the abolitionist movement, and the growing electoral power of the North.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Civil_War","img":"img/world/american-civil-war.jpg","thumb":"img/world/american-civil-war.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Nathaniel Currier and James Merritt Ives","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Gettysburg,_by_Currier_and_Ives.png"}},"Eiffel Tower":{"extract":"The Eiffel Tower (  EYE-fəl; French: Tour Eiffel [tuʁ ɛfɛl] ) is a lattice tower on the Champ de Mars in Paris, France. It is named after the engineer Gustave Eiffel, whose company designed and built the tower from 1887 to 1889.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eiffel_Tower","img":"img/world/eiffel-tower.jpg","thumb":"img/world/eiffel-tower.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Benh LIEU SONG","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tour_Eiffel_Wikimedia_Commons_(cropped).jpg"}},"Ellis Island":{"extract":"Ellis Island is an island in New York Harbor, within the U.S. states of New Jersey and New York. Owned by the U.S. government, Ellis Island was the busiest immigrant inspection and processing station in the United States.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ellis_Island","img":"img/world/ellis-island.jpg","thumb":"img/world/ellis-island.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Bain News Service, publisher","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arriving_at_Ellis_Island_LCCN2014710704.jpg"}},"Statue of Liberty":{"extract":"The Statue of Liberty (Liberty Enlightening the World, French: La Liberté éclairant le monde) is a colossal neoclassical sculpture of a robed and crowned woman located on Liberty Island, part of New York City, in New York Harbor. The copper-clad statue, a gift to the United States from the people of France, was designed by French sculptor Frédéric Auguste Bartholdi, and its metal framework built by Gustave Eiffel.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Statue_of_Liberty","img":"img/world/statue-of-liberty.jpg","thumb":"img/world/statue-of-liberty.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"AskALotl","license":"CC0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Front_view_of_Statue_of_Liberty_(cropped).jpg"}},"On the Origin of Species":{"extract":"On the Origin of Species by Means of Natural Selection, or the Preservation of Favoured Races in the Struggle for Life is a work of scientific literature by the English naturalist Charles Darwin that is considered to be the foundation of evolutionary biology. It was published on 24 November 1859.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/On_the_Origin_of_Species","img":"img/world/on-the-origin-of-species.jpg","thumb":"img/world/on-the-origin-of-species.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"John Murray, Publisher","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Origin_of_Species_title_page.jpg"}},"Nineteenth Amendment to the United States Constitution":{"extract":"The Nineteenth Amendment (Amendment XIX) to the United States Constitution prohibits the United States and its states from denying the right to vote to citizens of the United States on the basis of sex, in effect recognizing the right of women to vote. The amendment was the culmination of a decades-long movement for women's suffrage in the United States, at both the state and national levels, and was part of the worldwide movement towards women's suffrage and part of the wider women's rights movement.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nineteenth_Amendment_to_the_United_States_Constitution","img":"img/world/nineteenth-amendment-to-the-united-states-constitution.jpg","thumb":"img/world/nineteenth-amendment-to-the-united-states-constitution.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Ssolbergj","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greater_coat_of_arms_of_the_United_States.svg"}},"World War II":{"extract":"World War II, or the Second World War (1 September 1939 – 2 September 1945), was a global conflict between two coalitions: the Allies and the Axis powers. Nearly all of the world's countries were involved, with many engaging in total war on an unprecedented scale.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_War_II","img":"img/world/world-war-ii.jpg","thumb":"img/world/world-war-ii.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"The original uploader was Taak at English Wikipedia. Later versions were uploade","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Approaching_Omaha.jpg"}},"First transcontinental railroad":{"extract":"America's first transcontinental railroad (known originally as the \"Pacific Railroad\" and later as the \"Overland Route\") was a 1,911-mile (3,075 km) continuous railroad line built between 1863 and 1869 that connected the existing eastern U.S. rail network at Council Bluffs, Iowa, with the Pacific coast at the Oakland Long Wharf on San Francisco Bay. The rail line was built by three private companies over public lands provided by extensive U.S. land grants.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_transcontinental_railroad","img":"img/world/first-transcontinental-railroad.jpg","thumb":"img/world/first-transcontinental-railroad.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Andrew J. Russell / Adam Cuerden","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:East_and_West_Shaking_hands_at_the_laying_of_last_rail_Union_Pacific_Railroad_-_Restoration.jpg"}},"Incandescent light bulb":{"extract":"An incandescent light bulb, also known as an incandescent lamp or incandescent light globe, is an electric light that produces illumination by Joule heating a filament until it glows. The filament is enclosed in a glass bulb that is either evacuated or filled with inert gas to protect the filament from oxidation.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Incandescent_light_bulb","img":"img/world/incandescent-light-bulb.jpg","thumb":"img/world/incandescent-light-bulb.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"William J. Hammer","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edison_incandescent_lights.jpg"}},"1896 Summer Olympics":{"extract":"The 1896 Summer Olympics, officially known as the Games of the I Olympiad, and commonly known as Athens 1896 (Greek: Αθήνα 1896), were the first international Olympic Games held in modern history. Organised by the International Olympic Committee (IOC), which had been created by French aristocrat Pierre de Coubertin, the event was held in Athens, Greece, from 6 to 15 April 1896.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1896_Summer_Olympics","img":"img/world/1896-summer-olympics.jpg","thumb":"img/world/1896-summer-olympics.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Émile Gilliéron","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Commemorative_album_of_1896_Olympic_Games_-_cover_by_E._Gilli%C3%A9ron.jpg"}},"Dust Bowl":{"extract":"The Dust Bowl (1930–1940) was a period of severe dust storms and arid weather conditions that greatly damaged the agriculture and ecology of the High Plains and North American prairies. In the United States, it primarily affected farmland in western Kansas, the Oklahoma and Texas panhandles, southeast Colorado, and northeast New Mexico.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dust_Bowl","img":"img/world/dust-bowl.jpg","thumb":"img/world/dust-bowl.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Arthur Rothstein","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Farmer_walking_in_dust_storm_Cimarron_County_Oklahoma2.jpg"}},"The Crystal Palace":{"extract":"The Crystal Palace was a cast iron and plate glass structure, originally built in Hyde Park, London, to house the Great Exhibition of 1851. The exhibition took place from 1 May to 15 October 1851, and more than 14,000 exhibitors from around the world gathered in its 990,000-square-foot (92,000 m2) exhibition space to display examples of technology developed in the Industrial Revolution.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Crystal_Palace","img":"img/world/the-crystal-palace.jpg","thumb":"img/world/the-crystal-palace.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Philip Henry Delamotte (1821–1889)","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crystal_Palace_General_view_from_Water_Temple.jpg"}},"Ford Model T":{"extract":"The Ford Model T is an automobile that was produced by the Ford Motor Company from October 1, 1908, to May 26, 1927. It is generally regarded as the first mass-affordable automobile, which made car travel available to middle-class Americans.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ford_Model_T","img":"img/world/ford-model-t.jpg","thumb":"img/world/ford-model-t.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"ModelTMitch","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1925_Ford_Model_T_touring.jpg"}},"Wall Street crash of 1929":{"extract":"The Wall Street crash of 1929, also known as the Great Crash, was a major stock market crash in the United States which began in October 1929 with a sharp decline in prices on the New York Stock Exchange (NYSE). It triggered a rapid erosion of confidence in the U.S. banking system and marked what would later cascade into the worldwide Great Depression that lasted until the United States entered into World War II on December 8, 1941, making it the most devastating crash in the country's history.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wall_Street_crash_of_1929","img":"img/world/wall-street-crash-of-1929.jpg","thumb":"img/world/wall-street-crash-of-1929.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"US-gov","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crowd_outside_nyse.jpg"}},"Spanish flu":{"extract":"The 1918–1920 flu pandemic, also known as the Great Influenza epidemic or by the misleading name Spanish flu, was an exceptionally deadly global influenza pandemic caused by the H1N1 subtype of the influenza A virus. The earliest probable cases were documented in March 1918 in Haskell County, Kansas, United States, with further cases recorded in France, Germany and the United Kingdom in April.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spanish_flu","img":"img/world/spanish-flu.jpg","thumb":"img/world/spanish-flu.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Otis Historical Archives, National Museum of Health and Medicine","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emergency_hospital_during_Influenza_epidemic,_Camp_Funston,_Kansas_-_NCP_1603.jpg"}},"Spirit of St. Louis":{"extract":"The Spirit of St. Louis (formally the Ryan NYP; registered N-X-211) is the custom-built, single-engine, single-seat, high-wing monoplane that Charles Lindbergh flew on May 20–21, 1927, on the first solo nonstop transatlantic flight from Long Island, New York, to Paris, France, for which Lindbergh won the $25,000 Orteig Prize.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spirit_of_St._Louis","img":"img/world/spirit-of-st-louis.jpg","thumb":"img/world/spirit-of-st-louis.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Unknown authorUnknown author","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Lindbergh_and_the_Spirit_of_Saint_Louis_(Crisco_restoration,_with_wings).jpg"}},"Wright Flyer":{"extract":"The Wright Flyer (also known as the Kitty Hawk, Flyer I or the 1903 Flyer) made the first sustained flight by a manned heavier-than-air powered and controlled aircraft on December 17, 1903. Invented and flown by brothers Orville and Wilbur Wright, it marked the beginning of the pioneer era of aviation.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wright_Flyer","img":"img/world/wright-flyer.jpg","thumb":"img/world/wright-flyer.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"John T. Daniels","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_flight2.jpg"}},"Invention of the telephone":{"extract":"The invention of the telephone was the culmination of work done by many different people, and led to an array of lawsuits relating to the conflicting patent claims made by several individuals and numerous companies. Notable people included in this process were Antonio Meucci, Philipp Reis, Elisha Gray and Alexander Graham Bell.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Invention_of_the_telephone","img":"img/world/invention-of-the-telephone.jpg","thumb":"img/world/invention-of-the-telephone.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Alexander Graham Bell","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AGBell_Notebook.jpg"}},"World War I":{"extract":"World War I, or the First World War (28 July 1914 – 11 November 1918), also known as the Great War, was a global conflict between two coalitions: the Allies (or Entente) and the Central Powers. One of the deadliest conflicts in history, World War I caused 15 to 22 million deaths, including those resulting from war crimes and genocides.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_War_I","img":"img/world/world-war-i.jpg","thumb":"img/world/world-war-i.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Collection DocAnciens/docpix.fr","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_de_Verdun_1916.jpg"}},"Sinking of the Titanic":{"extract":"RMS Titanic sank in the North Atlantic Ocean on 15 April 1912. The largest ocean liner in service at the time, Titanic was four days into her maiden voyage from Southampton, England, to New York City, United States, with over 2,200 people on board when she struck an iceberg at 23:40 (ship's time) on 14 April.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sinking_of_the_Titanic","img":"img/world/sinking-of-the-titanic.jpg","thumb":"img/world/sinking-of-the-titanic.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Willy Stöwer","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St%C3%B6wer_Titanic.jpg"}},"Apollo 11":{"extract":"Apollo 11 (July 16–24, 1969) was the American spaceflight that first landed humans on the Moon, and the fifth crewed mission of NASA's Apollo program. The mission was crewed by Commander Neil Armstrong, Command Module Pilot Michael Collins, and Lunar Module Pilot Edwin \"Buzz\" Aldrin, all of whom were on their second and final spaceflight.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11","img":"img/world/apollo-11.jpg","thumb":"img/world/apollo-11.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Neil A. Armstrong","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Man_on_the_Moon,_AS11-40-5903_(cropped).jpg"}},"IBM Personal Computer":{"extract":"The IBM Personal Computer (model 5150), often referred to as the IBM PC, is the first microcomputer released in the IBM PC model line and the basis for the IBM PC compatible de facto standard. Released on August 12, 1981, it was created by a team of engineers and designers at International Business Machines (IBM), directed by William C. Lowe and Philip Don Estridge in Boca Raton, Florida.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_Personal_Computer","img":"img/world/ibm-personal-computer.jpg","thumb":"img/world/ibm-personal-computer.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Own work (based on graphics by International Business Machines Corporation)","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IBM_Personal_Computer_badge_recreation.svg"}},"Sputnik 1":{"extract":"Sputnik 1  (, Russian: Спутник-1, Satellite 1), often referred to as simply Sputnik, was the first artificial Earth satellite. It was launched into an elliptical low Earth orbit by the Soviet Union on 4 October 1957 as part of the Soviet space program.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sputnik_1"},"World Wide Web":{"extract":"The World Wide Web (also known as WWW, W3, or simply the Web) is a global interconnected information system that enables content sharing over the Internet. It facilitates access to documents and other web resources according to specific rules of the Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP).","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Wide_Web","img":"img/world/world-wide-web.jpg","thumb":"img/world/world-wide-web.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Harrison Schmitt / Apollo 17","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Blue_Marble,_AS17-148-22727.jpg"}},"Indian independence movement":{"extract":"The Indian independence movement was a series of political efforts from the mid-1880s to 1947 that took place in the Indian subcontinent with the aim of ending British colonial rule.\nThe first nationalistic movement took root when the Indian National Congress (INC) was formed in 1885.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_independence_movement","img":"img/world/indian-independence-movement.jpg","thumb":"img/world/indian-independence-movement.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"British Information Services, an agency of the British Government, restoration b","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:India,_by_British_Information_Services,_1944_restored_version.jpg"}},"March on Washington for Jobs and Freedom":{"extract":"The March on Washington for Jobs and Freedom (commonly known as the March on Washington or the Great March on Washington) was held in Washington, D.C., on August 28, 1963. The purpose of the march was to advocate for the civil and economic rights of African Americans.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/March_on_Washington","img":"img/world/march-on-washington-for-jobs-and-freedom.jpg","thumb":"img/world/march-on-washington-for-jobs-and-freedom.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"This photograph was made by Rowland Scherman at the March on Washington. The neg","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Civil_rights_march_on_Washington,_D.C._from_Lincoln_(cropped).jpg"}},"Hungarian Revolution of 1956":{"extract":"The Hungarian Revolution of 1956 (23 October – 4 November 1956; Hungarian: 1956-os forradalom), also known as the Hungarian Uprising, was an attempted countrywide revolution against the government of the Hungarian People's Republic (1949–1989) and the policies caused by the government's subordination to the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics (USSR). The uprising lasted 15 days before being crushed by Soviet troops on 7 November 1956 (outside of Budapest, firefights lasted until at least 12 November 1956).","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hungarian_Revolution_of_1956","img":"img/world/hungarian-revolution-of-1956.jpg","thumb":"img/world/hungarian-revolution-of-1956.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Главцентроархив","license":"CC0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hungarian_Revolution_of_1956_collage.jpg"}},"IPhone (1st generation)":{"extract":"The iPhone is a smartphone developed and marketed by Apple as the first device in the iPhone lineup of smartphones. It features a Samsung S5L8900 SoC (90 nm), a 3.5 in multi-touch display, and a web browser (Safari).","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IPhone_(1st_generation)"},"1953 British Mount Everest expedition":{"extract":"The 1953 British Mount Everest expedition was the ninth mountaineering expedition to attempt the first ascent of Mount Everest, and the first confirmed to have succeeded when Tenzing Norgay and Edmund Hillary reached the summit on 29 May 1953 at 11:30 a.m. Led by Colonel John Hunt, it was organised and financed by the Joint Himalayan Committee.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1953_British_Mount_Everest_expedition"},"Fall of the Berlin Wall":{"extract":"The fall of the Berlin Wall along with the fall of the inner German border occurred after a Politburo press conference on 9 November 1989 prematurely announced that East Germans could travel without restrictions after, in the decades before, hundreds had been killed by Border Troops of the German Democratic Republic when trying to sneak through the Iron Curtain. \nSoon, people gathered at Berlin border crossings, citing the news that were broadcast in East and West, and puzzled border guards eventually let them pass in the late evening hours, with live television broadcasting worldwide.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fall_of_the_Berlin_Wall","img":"img/world/fall-of-the-berlin-wall.jpg","thumb":"img/world/fall-of-the-berlin-wall.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"SSGT F. Lee Corkran","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crane_removed_part_of_Wall_Brandenburg_Gate.jpg"}},"COVID-19 pandemic":{"extract":"The global COVID-19 pandemic (also known as the coronavirus pandemic), caused by severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2), began with an outbreak in Wuhan, China, in December 2019. It spread to other parts of Asia and then worldwide in early 2020.","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19_pandemic","img":"img/world/covid-19-pandemic.jpg","thumb":"img/world/covid-19-pandemic.thumb.jpg","credit":{"artist":"Navy Medicine from Washington, DC, USA","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:USNS_Comfort_New_York_City_2020.jpg"}}};
